115
00:08:53,427 --> 00:08:57,330
[SPEAKER_05]: Abandon the assumption of sīla-pāra of past.

116
00:08:57,450 --> 00:08:59,451
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you do that?

117
00:09:00,411 --> 00:09:13,940
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it's understanding why the why wholesome is wholesome and why unwholesome is unwholesome, in the sense of, in relation to the... How do you do that?

118
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:17,422
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, I suppose this this idea of

119
00:09:17,982 --> 00:09:21,679
[SPEAKER_03]: If you start with purification of virtue, so you don't start with, you know...

120
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:27,473
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding of the dhamma and all the other things that will kind of provide you with the answers, you start with purification of virtue.

121
00:09:27,573 --> 00:09:31,596
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you abandon sīla-bhāta-paramāsa just by the means of virtue?

122
00:09:31,956 --> 00:09:32,197
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

123
00:09:32,797 --> 00:09:38,941
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that's why you have to have this final... Well, actually, you can.

124
00:09:39,542 --> 00:09:43,744
[SPEAKER_03]: The Buddha did say puthujjana can free himself from sīla-bhāta-paramāsa without a final goal.

125
00:09:45,005 --> 00:09:45,606
[SPEAKER_03]: It's an assumption.

126
00:09:46,566 --> 00:09:51,610
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is then that which a puthujjana can do, let alone somebody who knows the Buddha's teaching,

127
00:09:52,733 --> 00:10:07,105
[SPEAKER_03]: In regard to the keeping the precepts that will actually free him from sīlabhāta paramāsa, needing the tradition, needing the authority, needing the teacher, they would free him from any external kind of criteria he would see for himself, the purification of virtue and the accomplishment of it.

128
00:10:08,346 --> 00:10:13,830
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the single factor that's absent in the one who doesn't see it and present in the one who sees it?

129
00:10:14,871 --> 00:10:16,793
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding the intention behind.

130
00:10:17,734 --> 00:10:19,335
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the single factor behind that?

131
00:10:20,807 --> 00:10:22,128
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you understand intention?

132
00:10:22,148 --> 00:10:22,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry?

133
00:10:22,928 --> 00:10:25,069
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing... attending, correct?

134
00:10:26,110 --> 00:10:31,093
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, but even that requires the same responsibility.

135
00:10:31,693 --> 00:10:36,676
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to take responsibility for what you're doing, including taking on the precept.

136
00:10:36,756 --> 00:10:40,938
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not an external commandment to any, to the slightest degree, it's not.

137
00:10:42,742 --> 00:10:52,570
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you need external authority, traditions, senior agents or whatever, to keep you together from breaking precepts, you're not purifying anything.

138
00:10:53,451 --> 00:10:59,896
[SPEAKER_03]: You just have enough foresight to place yourself in the environment that will force you to not misbehave.

139
00:11:00,417 --> 00:11:02,358
[SPEAKER_03]: But even that, it's only going to be relative.

140
00:11:03,699 --> 00:11:07,983
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you need to be doing it yourself and take responsibility for that.

141
00:11:10,045 --> 00:11:22,729
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm keeping the precepts because I want to purify the virtue, because I want to purify the mind, because I want to purify the views, the doubt, the knowledge and vision of what's path, what it isn't path, the knowledge of the path, the nibbāna.

142
00:11:23,249 --> 00:11:25,450
[SPEAKER_03]: I want to be free from suffering once and for all.

143
00:11:26,970 --> 00:11:29,451
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, if Ajahn comes and says, no, no, you can do that.

144
00:11:29,491 --> 00:11:30,731
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not against the vinaya.

145
00:11:31,251 --> 00:11:32,892
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not a criteria for you to do it.

146
00:11:33,741 --> 00:11:38,507
[SPEAKER_03]: If Ajahn comes and says, oh, you mustn't do that, well, that's again not a criteria for you to not do it.

147
00:11:39,168 --> 00:11:46,336
[SPEAKER_03]: The criteria is the knowledge of the precepts and then that exactly responsibility for keeping the precepts is taken.

148
00:11:46,677 --> 00:11:47,918
[SPEAKER_03]: This is why I'm doing it.

149
00:11:48,699 --> 00:11:50,181
[SPEAKER_03]: You're gonna feel the kind of the

150
00:11:51,214 --> 00:11:55,455
[SPEAKER_03]: The closeness of the possibility of, oh, so I could break it with no repercussions.

151
00:11:56,055 --> 00:11:56,675
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, exactly.

152
00:11:56,716 --> 00:12:06,118
[SPEAKER_03]: That's when you need to start contemplating the danger and really consider, like, okay, but am I doing... Why do I even want to practice in the first place?

153
00:12:07,058 --> 00:12:08,039
[SPEAKER_03]: What is my motivation?

154
00:12:08,079 --> 00:12:08,899
[SPEAKER_03]: What are my reasons?

155
00:12:09,799 --> 00:12:12,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Because according to what I want to practice, i.e.

156
00:12:13,020 --> 00:12:16,521
[SPEAKER_03]: the Buddha's instruction, I should be able to do it by myself.

157
00:12:17,218 --> 00:12:36,876
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it concerns only me, and if I need an external authority, tradition, the way how things were done, or some very, like, inspiring, you know, non-compromising teacher to keep me together, well, that is... that is no practice.

158
00:12:38,311 --> 00:12:38,571
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure.

159
00:12:39,252 --> 00:12:42,554
[SPEAKER_03]: It's... if somebody has nothing, and that can make them keep the precepts, fine.

160
00:12:42,755 --> 00:12:44,936
[SPEAKER_03]: That's better than not keeping the precepts at all.

161
00:12:45,637 --> 00:12:48,499
[SPEAKER_03]: But in and of itself, that's exactly what's still about the Paramahasaya is.

162
00:12:48,839 --> 00:12:57,666
[SPEAKER_03]: Precepts have been taken, duty has been taken, but responsibility for it has been covered up, avoided, dismissed.

163
00:12:57,726 --> 00:13:02,490
[SPEAKER_03]: So, no purification of virtue means there will be no samādhi, even if you keep the precepts for 100 years.

164
00:13:05,504 --> 00:13:22,331
[SPEAKER_03]: And consequently, also, purifying the virtue means there will be samādhi, even if you don't do any meditation techniques, which usually go hand in hand with not purifying the virtue, but keeping it on account of authority, or like a secondary kind of, okay, yeah, do this, because it's probably good for you on some level.

165
00:13:23,311 --> 00:13:29,294
[SPEAKER_03]: But really, really, the meat of the practice is sensation watching, or nostril observation, or belly bhavana, or whatever else.

166
00:13:30,755 --> 00:13:31,455
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, no.

167
00:13:31,876 --> 00:13:33,416
[SPEAKER_03]: First chariot leads you to the second one.

168
00:13:34,017 --> 00:13:36,318
[SPEAKER_03]: Factual samādhi, factual purification of the mind.

169
00:13:37,078 --> 00:13:41,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you purify your mind through the concentration practices and focusing and ignoring what bothers you?

170
00:13:42,902 --> 00:13:45,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, not according to the Buddha.

171
00:13:45,483 --> 00:13:53,667
[SPEAKER_03]: You purify your mind by purifying your intentions, that are now revealed on account of having traveled with the first chariot as far as it goes.

172
00:13:54,928 --> 00:13:58,570
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, on that subtle mental level, the intention of greed,

173
00:13:59,251 --> 00:13:59,772
[SPEAKER_03]: Non-greed.

174
00:14:00,293 --> 00:14:01,816
[SPEAKER_03]: Aversion, non-aversion.

175
00:14:02,036 --> 00:14:03,539
[SPEAKER_03]: Distraction, non-distraction.

176
00:14:03,579 --> 00:14:04,161
[SPEAKER_03]: That's that.

177
00:14:04,221 --> 00:14:04,802
[SPEAKER_03]: That's apparent.

178
00:14:05,022 --> 00:14:05,543
[SPEAKER_03]: That's seen.

179
00:14:06,325 --> 00:14:11,575
[SPEAKER_03]: That's as seen as the first, you know, crude, coarse precepts were in the beginning.

180
00:14:13,612 --> 00:14:24,900
[SPEAKER_03]: So, whatever you're about to do, as the Buddha instructed Rāhula in that sutta, like with a similar mirror, well, you reflect against it, on having purified the virtue, not just pontificate around it.

181
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:30,624
[SPEAKER_03]: Having purified the virtue, you have intention to do this or that, say this or that, think this or that.

182
00:14:30,684 --> 00:14:34,046
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, is this leading to my harm, harm of others?

183
00:14:34,126 --> 00:14:36,988
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it making the greed increase or decrease?

184
00:14:37,268 --> 00:14:39,330
[SPEAKER_03]: An honour is in greed, come or not come.

185
00:14:39,890 --> 00:14:40,711
[SPEAKER_03]: Same for aversion.

186
00:14:41,927 --> 00:14:57,334
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you gotta... now, the traveling of the... with the second chariot, purification of the mind entails the purification and the clarity of the context of what's beneficial truly and what it's not truly beneficial.

187
00:14:59,175 --> 00:15:01,436
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is determined by where the intentions are rooted.

188
00:15:03,217 --> 00:15:05,458
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is factual samādhi.

189
00:15:05,958 --> 00:15:07,519
[SPEAKER_03]: That is factual imperturbability.

190
00:15:09,215 --> 00:15:13,116
[SPEAKER_01]: No need for focusing, no need for concentration, just wisdom and context.

191
00:15:13,596 --> 00:15:14,616
[SPEAKER_01]: I know, big surprise.

192
00:15:16,636 --> 00:15:31,279
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, inevitably, if you have been purifying your intentions, not acting out of the unwholesome ones, it will lead you to the purification of the views.

193
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:35,620
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you purify the views then?

194
00:15:55,412 --> 00:16:08,023
[SPEAKER_05]: By re-establishing the one's own understanding of... Of what?

195
00:16:09,344 --> 00:16:10,044
[SPEAKER_03]: What specifically?

196
00:16:12,286 --> 00:16:20,393
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, how do you... how do you know you're traveling by a third chariot?

197
00:16:35,195 --> 00:16:38,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

198
00:16:39,159 --> 00:16:43,783
[SPEAKER_03]: But, I mean, like, literally, how do you know that you're in a third chariot, if you're in a chariot?

199
00:16:46,305 --> 00:16:49,548
[SPEAKER_03]: By remembering that you traveled in the first one, and then the second one.

200
00:16:50,188 --> 00:16:54,913
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the principle of the first one, the principle of the second one, leads you to the principle of the third one.

201
00:16:55,833 --> 00:16:59,637
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, you, by that, by the time you're in a third chariot,

202
00:17:00,438 --> 00:17:07,822
[SPEAKER_03]: You will pretty much already know what's important and what is not important, what constitutes the successful travel and what constitutes unsuccessful travel.

203
00:17:08,762 --> 00:17:19,948
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh... views... would then... any views that you... any views that might come up, would be already seen, to a degree, against that which you've been doing.

204
00:17:21,751 --> 00:17:29,700
[SPEAKER_03]: So, and that's why, as I said, like, if you put aside this specific sutta, sīla-bhāt-paramāsa, the purification virtue, can be done by somebody even with the wrong views.

205
00:17:29,881 --> 00:17:35,127
[SPEAKER_03]: If they put the views aside, whatever views they have, they don't emphasize that.

206
00:17:35,587 --> 00:17:41,555
[SPEAKER_03]: They emphasize taking on responsibility for behavior by body, speech, intentions of the mind.

207
00:17:42,322 --> 00:17:44,045
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they might have had the views still.

208
00:17:44,125 --> 00:17:44,866
[SPEAKER_03]: I was in the past.

209
00:17:44,926 --> 00:17:45,487
[SPEAKER_03]: I was like this.

210
00:17:45,547 --> 00:17:47,089
[SPEAKER_03]: Will I be... Nibbāna is that.

211
00:17:47,189 --> 00:17:48,051
[SPEAKER_03]: This is the practice.

212
00:17:48,071 --> 00:17:48,952
[SPEAKER_03]: This is not the practice.

213
00:17:49,433 --> 00:17:55,221
[SPEAKER_03]: But regardless of what thinking they've been having, what views they've been revolving around, they did not emphasize that.

214
00:17:55,942 --> 00:17:58,105
[SPEAKER_03]: They actually emphasized doing the restraint.

215
00:17:58,626 --> 00:18:10,435
[SPEAKER_03]: Keeping the precepts, sensory strain, guarding, and taking responsibility for it, and enduring the weight of that responsibility, not just sort of misplacing it back onto the... oh, yeah, no, no, we're in this together, that's why we do this, and so on.

216
00:18:10,455 --> 00:18:12,677
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you feel... you feel lighter on account of it.

217
00:18:13,858 --> 00:18:16,700
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if they actually have been putting their views aside,

218
00:18:18,097 --> 00:18:24,580
[SPEAKER_03]: And practice the purification of virtue and the purification of the mind, that in itself already constitutes the purification of the views.

219
00:18:25,421 --> 00:18:32,184
[SPEAKER_03]: So now, when the views would come up, more specifically, well, they would already know not to put the emphasis on them.

220
00:18:32,884 --> 00:18:34,725
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how you purify the views.

221
00:18:34,765 --> 00:18:38,167
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly why that leads you to going beyond doubt.

222
00:18:39,848 --> 00:18:43,350
[SPEAKER_03]: Being unable to emphasize what cannot be emphasized.

223
00:18:43,970 --> 00:18:47,712
[SPEAKER_03]: Being unable to pervert your... being unable to doubt that which precedes the doubt.

224
00:18:48,702 --> 00:18:49,782
[SPEAKER_03]: Whichever way you want to put it.

225
00:18:52,183 --> 00:18:54,204
[SPEAKER_03]: So, oh, how do I become a Sotāpanna?

226
00:18:54,224 --> 00:18:54,684
[SPEAKER_03]: What do I do?

227
00:18:54,704 --> 00:18:55,684
[SPEAKER_03]: What technique do I follow?

228
00:18:55,704 --> 00:18:57,985
[SPEAKER_03]: What nāma, rūpa, viññāṇa, relationship I need to understand?

229
00:18:58,965 --> 00:19:05,067
[SPEAKER_03]: Purify the virtue, purify your intentions, that will undo your views, that will make you go beyond doubt.

230
00:19:08,327 --> 00:19:08,708
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

231
00:19:10,588 --> 00:19:17,230
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, exactly, the next chariot is, oh, he knows what the practice is and what the practice isn't.

232
00:19:18,185 --> 00:19:20,967
[SPEAKER_03]: He knows exactly what it would lead to nibbāna and what wouldn't.

233
00:19:21,467 --> 00:19:22,468
[SPEAKER_03]: He knows for himself.

234
00:19:22,849 --> 00:19:23,349
[SPEAKER_03]: He sees it.

235
00:19:26,031 --> 00:19:31,695
[SPEAKER_03]: And the next chariot, if he follows that which leads to nibbāna, well, he knows what nibbāna is now.

236
00:19:31,855 --> 00:19:32,396
[SPEAKER_03]: It's right there.

237
00:19:33,316 --> 00:19:34,237
[SPEAKER_03]: The knowledge and vision.

238
00:19:42,103 --> 00:19:43,244
[SPEAKER_03]: And this is important because

239
00:19:44,831 --> 00:19:48,355
[SPEAKER_03]: The views come first in everyone's experience.

240
00:19:50,797 --> 00:19:51,718
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't change that.

241
00:19:51,898 --> 00:19:56,042
[SPEAKER_03]: People would hope, okay, putting the views aside means these views will stop coming up for me.

242
00:19:56,203 --> 00:19:56,663
[SPEAKER_02]: No, they won't.

243
00:19:58,365 --> 00:20:03,470
[SPEAKER_03]: But you can use your discernment to not allow yourself to emphasize them and act out of them.

244
00:20:04,552 --> 00:20:10,274
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, yeah, I think it's like this, or I think it's like that, I think it might be like this, but... Am I purifying the virtue?

245
00:20:10,294 --> 00:20:12,054
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I keeping the precepts, guarding all the sense doors?

246
00:20:12,434 --> 00:20:13,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Have I taken responsibility?

247
00:20:14,035 --> 00:20:21,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I feeling the weight of my investment, so to speak, in this practice?

248
00:20:22,158 --> 00:20:25,018
[SPEAKER_03]: Or do I feel absolved because I found a greater, bigger thing?

249
00:20:25,058 --> 00:20:25,839
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this community?

250
00:20:25,859 --> 00:20:26,699
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this religion?

251
00:20:26,759 --> 00:20:27,499
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this practice?

252
00:20:27,559 --> 00:20:28,560
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this meditation center?

253
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:29,240
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this technique?

254
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:29,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this whatever?

255
00:20:32,638 --> 00:20:36,422
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the danger isn't in there being these views.

256
00:20:36,562 --> 00:20:39,145
[SPEAKER_05]: The danger is sort of becoming intoxicated by them.

257
00:20:39,245 --> 00:20:40,246
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

258
00:20:40,927 --> 00:20:46,692
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, the danger is allowing yourself to shift back the emphasis onto the views.

259
00:20:47,593 --> 00:20:57,242
[SPEAKER_03]: And kind of, oh, yes, yes, you know, vinaya and precepts, sure, you know, but really drift away from the... from the... from the... from the emphasis.

260
00:20:57,322 --> 00:20:58,603
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, from the emphasis on that.

261
00:20:59,224 --> 00:21:05,510
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is, you know, you hear that, like you would see that, you know, people are like, yeah, yeah, eight precepts and stuff, but that's it.

262
00:21:06,472 --> 00:21:07,833
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they're kind of brushed aside.

263
00:21:08,433 --> 00:21:12,195
[SPEAKER_03]: This is the factual thing, obvious to everyone, keeping the precepts of virtue.

264
00:21:13,276 --> 00:21:13,936
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, listen.

265
00:21:13,976 --> 00:21:14,797
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the practice.

266
00:21:15,677 --> 00:21:18,139
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's just going to be now information about the views.

267
00:21:18,759 --> 00:21:19,099
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

268
00:21:19,399 --> 00:21:23,301
[SPEAKER_03]: When you hear people describe the practice, it's just people describing views and opinions.

269
00:21:24,842 --> 00:21:31,166
[SPEAKER_03]: As towards this, which provides information, satisfying answer, not satisfying answer, but that's it.

270
00:21:32,220 --> 00:21:37,281
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, yeah, okay, but how do I purify this virtue then, that then leads me to the purification of the mind?

271
00:21:39,542 --> 00:21:40,702
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I purify these views?

272
00:21:40,863 --> 00:21:44,023
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, I can take it or leave it, sure, but what do I do?

273
00:21:44,063 --> 00:21:50,185
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you know, consciousness never changes, it's always there, you just need to see it, be here now.

274
00:21:50,205 --> 00:21:51,426
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, but how do I do that?

275
00:21:52,286 --> 00:21:55,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, by seeing that consciousness is unchanging, by being here.

276
00:21:56,407 --> 00:21:58,188
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you're just saying the same thing in different words.

277
00:21:58,468 --> 00:21:59,248
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I do it?

278
00:21:59,898 --> 00:22:00,458
[SPEAKER_03]: What do I do?

279
00:22:00,999 --> 00:22:03,020
[SPEAKER_03]: Where are these chariots that I need to travel with?

280
00:22:03,261 --> 00:22:05,882
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the same when it comes to when people quote the suttas.

281
00:22:05,902 --> 00:22:09,105
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, yeah, see, anatta, it's anatta in this, anatta in that, anatta...

282
00:22:09,665 --> 00:22:10,466
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so how do you do it?

283
00:22:10,486 --> 00:22:11,547
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, by seeing no self.

284
00:22:11,887 --> 00:22:13,628
[SPEAKER_03]: But how do I do that?

285
00:22:13,668 --> 00:22:14,969
[SPEAKER_03]: By seeing that you can't own things.

286
00:22:15,910 --> 00:22:18,232
[SPEAKER_03]: Again, you're just giving the synonyms of the same thing.

287
00:22:18,592 --> 00:22:19,413
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I do it?

288
00:22:20,193 --> 00:22:24,777
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the Buddha didn't just come out of the blue and just said, listen, there's no self.

289
00:22:25,477 --> 00:22:25,937
[SPEAKER_03]: You're done.

290
00:22:27,899 --> 00:22:28,940
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole gradual training.

291
00:22:30,499 --> 00:22:31,360
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so precepts.

292
00:22:31,380 --> 00:22:32,040
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what do you do?

293
00:22:32,060 --> 00:22:32,881
[SPEAKER_03]: Just keep the precepts?

294
00:22:32,941 --> 00:22:33,121
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

295
00:22:33,721 --> 00:22:34,662
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the next step now?

296
00:22:34,942 --> 00:22:38,445
[SPEAKER_03]: Will the next step be on the level of, well, this is how you purify the precepts further?

297
00:22:39,326 --> 00:22:40,967
[SPEAKER_03]: This is how you then see intention of your mind?

298
00:22:40,987 --> 00:22:43,148
[SPEAKER_03]: Or just like, oh, well, yeah, you keep the precepts.

299
00:22:43,208 --> 00:22:55,918
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, relax, watch your nostrils, get your kāmatana, focus on the objects, concentrate and concentrate and concentrate, and everything else will just... nibbāna, vijjāna,

300
00:22:56,939 --> 00:23:00,842
[SPEAKER_03]: A diligent monk, does he sit watching his nostrils, feeling blissful?

301
00:23:01,423 --> 00:23:01,563
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

302
00:23:01,783 --> 00:23:10,510
[SPEAKER_03]: He purifies his mind on account of developing four foundations of mindfulness, and then he feels blissful, because he's deathless, because he's free from prison, his suffering cannot touch him.

303
00:23:10,971 --> 00:23:19,137
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, even then, as I said, when we started this, even the whole... like, he recollects his unbroken virtue, for which he'd taken responsibility,

304
00:23:19,918 --> 00:23:24,382
[SPEAKER_03]: And he knows now, because he's taken responsibility, that he cannot break it.

305
00:23:24,963 --> 00:23:27,525
[SPEAKER_03]: He'll never know that if he hasn't taken responsibility.

306
00:23:27,805 --> 00:23:33,090
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, well, I'm not breaking it here because these ājāns, you know, will kick me out and so on.

307
00:23:34,831 --> 00:23:36,433
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, but what if my circumstances change?

308
00:23:38,023 --> 00:23:39,083
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I know for sure then?

309
00:23:39,483 --> 00:23:48,225
[SPEAKER_03]: When there is nobody to punish me or tell me off or fear or blame or something like that in this environment, if there is no such thing there, do I know for sure?

310
00:23:48,485 --> 00:23:49,626
[SPEAKER_03]: I will still not do this.

311
00:23:52,246 --> 00:23:55,867
[SPEAKER_03]: Or if the circumstances change slightly, oh, I don't know.

312
00:23:56,927 --> 00:23:58,808
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, what purification have you done?

313
00:23:59,148 --> 00:23:59,348
[SPEAKER_02]: None.

314
00:24:00,870 --> 00:24:04,434
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a guy in prison who can't do, can't kill people.

315
00:24:04,454 --> 00:24:04,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

316
00:24:05,535 --> 00:24:11,001
[SPEAKER_03]: As I said, circumstantially, it's better than allowing it to be done, but we're not talking about that.

317
00:24:11,041 --> 00:24:14,324
[SPEAKER_03]: That's like how layman should live his life, not the one who wants to practice.

318
00:24:16,026 --> 00:24:21,251
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the point is then, oh, if you've taken responsibility, the time, if you have invested effort, time will come.

319
00:24:21,391 --> 00:24:21,852
[SPEAKER_03]: He knows.

320
00:24:23,264 --> 00:24:38,053
[SPEAKER_03]: No matter the circumstances that are not in my control, what comes my way, what temptation, what pressure, what kind of things, discomforts, discontentment, accomplished in virtue means knowing that he will not do anything against their virtue.

321
00:24:38,813 --> 00:24:41,354
[SPEAKER_03]: And that gives rise to pleasant abiding here and now.

322
00:24:42,775 --> 00:24:43,456
[SPEAKER_05]: The samādhi.

323
00:24:43,556 --> 00:24:48,959
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's also this responsibility, I think, initially,

324
00:24:50,614 --> 00:24:51,635
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, painful.

325
00:24:51,655 --> 00:24:52,776
[SPEAKER_05]: So, so people... Of course.

326
00:24:53,197 --> 00:25:09,652
[SPEAKER_03]: People who will... Well, there is a reason that people would willingly go into into a factor of sīla bhārata paramaṣa, willingly put it on there, because it actually, at this kind of immediate level, it makes it more pleasant for me, or makes it less unpleasant.

327
00:25:09,672 --> 00:25:10,493
[SPEAKER_03]: But in the long run,

328
00:25:11,953 --> 00:25:12,933
[SPEAKER_05]: That prevents your progress.

329
00:25:13,174 --> 00:25:20,177
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's why I will give so much faith to this external form or this meditation technique.

330
00:25:20,297 --> 00:25:23,919
[SPEAKER_03]: And now that you mention faith, that's another equally misunderstood thing.

331
00:25:23,979 --> 00:25:33,283
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, faith means I'll just be brain-dead, devoted to the instruction of this person, as in inspiration that I get behind this instruction.

332
00:25:33,910 --> 00:25:34,070
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

333
00:25:34,310 --> 00:25:39,391
[SPEAKER_03]: Faith means, put your view second, keep the virtue, guard the sense doors first.

334
00:25:39,892 --> 00:25:42,132
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you won't see a direct reason to do it.

335
00:25:42,152 --> 00:25:43,613
[SPEAKER_03]: Your views are telling you otherwise.

336
00:25:44,233 --> 00:25:45,813
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole world is telling you otherwise.

337
00:25:47,234 --> 00:26:00,197
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you have enough faith in what you understood from what the Buddha instructed, so out of that faith, you don't revolve around, oh, what is nāmarūpa, and is this, and is that, and is that, oh, what is the etymology of this, what...

338
00:26:00,863 --> 00:26:01,023
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

339
00:26:01,423 --> 00:26:15,429
[SPEAKER_03]: You put all that aside, and you actually take responsibility for the virtue, and you got to sense those, and you pretty much maintain and develop and increase the wholesome context behind your intentions.

340
00:26:17,389 --> 00:26:17,870
[SPEAKER_03]: Out of faith.

341
00:26:18,990 --> 00:26:20,911
[SPEAKER_03]: You have no other reason other than faith.

342
00:26:21,071 --> 00:26:21,211
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

343
00:26:21,271 --> 00:26:25,512
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll do this because it makes sense to that extent, and I have faith that the Buddha was right.

344
00:26:26,053 --> 00:26:26,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me just

345
00:26:27,826 --> 00:26:37,072
[SPEAKER_03]: Start doing this, while the views and all the answers I seek, and all the doubts and insecurities, I'll just put that second until I deal with these things first.

346
00:26:37,573 --> 00:26:38,333
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the faith.

347
00:26:38,713 --> 00:26:43,697
[SPEAKER_03]: And you could see how that faith could equally result in sotāpatti and arahantship, because you're actually doing the work.

348
00:26:44,117 --> 00:26:47,179
[SPEAKER_05]: So it's very... that faith is very different to, like, inspiration.

349
00:26:47,419 --> 00:26:48,540
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

350
00:26:49,661 --> 00:26:54,164
[SPEAKER_00]: What I wanted to say as well is that about taking responsibility for virtue,

351
00:26:55,656 --> 00:27:06,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It very much also pertains to being able even to purify the views by putting views aside, because you have to also

352
00:27:08,131 --> 00:27:15,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Because views aren't usually something that you take responsibility for, or consciously even hold.

353
00:27:15,972 --> 00:27:17,113
[SPEAKER_00]: I think this, and I think that.

354
00:27:17,133 --> 00:27:28,175
[SPEAKER_00]: They're more just very fundamental, like assumptions about things that you take so much for granted, that you don't even take responsibility or realize that, you know, this is a view that I have.

355
00:27:28,675 --> 00:27:33,156
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it means I could... it might not be right, or I could not have it.

356
00:27:33,676 --> 00:27:33,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

357
00:27:34,976 --> 00:27:43,480
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's more subtle even than the responsibility for actions and things.

358
00:27:43,500 --> 00:27:51,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you kind of need to start with that, which then sort of shows you that, oh, even these views that I hold, I am responsible for holding them.

359
00:27:52,384 --> 00:27:55,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then let me...

360
00:27:55,125 --> 00:28:03,969
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't... let me put them aside, as you said, and continue to do this, which I see is beneficial, which will lead me in this direction.

361
00:28:04,874 --> 00:28:20,469
[SPEAKER_03]: By putting the views aside and purifying virtue and purifying your mind, it does actually start to create a reference point for those views, which is a new view, sure, but it's the right view.

362
00:28:20,690 --> 00:28:27,837
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the view that has arisen as a result of practice that has not been acting out of and emphasizing the views.

363
00:28:28,519 --> 00:28:30,161
[SPEAKER_05]: You can see them as views.

364
00:28:30,181 --> 00:28:30,761
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

365
00:28:30,821 --> 00:28:31,542
[SPEAKER_05]: You get to see them.

366
00:28:31,622 --> 00:28:33,584
[SPEAKER_05]: You're just blind to them being views.

367
00:28:33,624 --> 00:28:34,365
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

368
00:28:35,686 --> 00:28:39,030
[SPEAKER_03]: But that whole thing of, like, you cannot have the views, that's for sure.

369
00:28:39,690 --> 00:28:40,651
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't just drop them.

370
00:28:40,671 --> 00:28:41,973
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't just choose to not hold them.

371
00:28:42,674 --> 00:28:45,376
[SPEAKER_03]: But what you can do is choose to not prioritize them anymore.

372
00:28:46,275 --> 00:28:55,802
[SPEAKER_03]: Out of faith, purify your virtue, purify your mind, and that will then diminish these views, which gives rise to what the right view is.

373
00:28:56,243 --> 00:28:59,005
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, you would replace your wrong views with the right view.

374
00:29:00,506 --> 00:29:03,388
[SPEAKER_03]: A view that knows what the path is and what the path isn't.

375
00:29:03,889 --> 00:29:06,690
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like you also... you can't not have faith.

376
00:29:06,790 --> 00:29:18,234
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to have faith in something, whether it's drugs or escape or... Whatever you're finding your safety, whatever you're investing effort in maintaining your sense of safety, that is where you have your faith in.

377
00:29:18,995 --> 00:29:24,076
[SPEAKER_03]: Whatever that is, whether it's sensuality, tradition, authority, family, possessions...

378
00:29:24,096 --> 00:29:24,617
[SPEAKER_03]: It's inevitable.

379
00:29:24,977 --> 00:29:25,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

380
00:29:26,317 --> 00:29:27,958
[SPEAKER_03]: In as much as the views are inevitable.

381
00:29:28,783 --> 00:29:30,687
[SPEAKER_03]: A degree of faith and belief is inevitable.

382
00:29:31,108 --> 00:29:37,020
[SPEAKER_03]: But what is not inevitable is to lose your reason in regard to it, and not be aware of your situation within it.

383
00:29:39,268 --> 00:29:44,353
[SPEAKER_05]: So, and that will lead to faith, whereby that faith becomes wisdom?

384
00:29:44,373 --> 00:29:44,573
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

385
00:29:44,833 --> 00:29:46,274
[SPEAKER_03]: Faith, basically, informed.

386
00:29:46,555 --> 00:29:47,215
[SPEAKER_03]: Informed faith.

387
00:29:47,295 --> 00:29:52,900
[SPEAKER_03]: Not faith on account of emotional experience and feeling that I have when I listen to this or read that.

388
00:29:53,521 --> 00:29:53,981
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's the way.

389
00:29:54,001 --> 00:29:56,323
[SPEAKER_03]: What is factually good for me?

390
00:29:56,644 --> 00:30:01,348
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, to find a doctor who gives me an instruction, a recipe.

391
00:30:02,088 --> 00:30:04,190
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how will I know that's correct?

392
00:30:04,350 --> 00:30:05,531
[SPEAKER_03]: How will I know that will cure me?

393
00:30:05,712 --> 00:30:05,912
[SPEAKER_03]: Well,

394
00:30:07,367 --> 00:30:10,489
[SPEAKER_03]: By initially taking it on faith and beginning the application of it.

395
00:30:10,649 --> 00:30:17,534
[SPEAKER_03]: Adhering to a diet, protecting of the wound, not increasing the infection and so on, out of faith.

396
00:30:18,835 --> 00:30:25,540
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, as your as your health improves on account of it, you won't have that faith anymore, because you saw for yourself that it cured you.

397
00:30:27,041 --> 00:30:27,822
[SPEAKER_05]: But the faith...

398
00:30:29,286 --> 00:30:33,447
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't lead to the end of faith, is intoxicating.

399
00:30:33,487 --> 00:30:34,707
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's a wrong view.

400
00:30:34,948 --> 00:30:36,468
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's like...

401
00:30:36,968 --> 00:30:39,309
[SPEAKER_03]: It's basically a faith for which you haven't taken responsibility.

402
00:30:39,349 --> 00:30:46,491
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so I will continue to believe it because it's... Because it makes me feel better, makes me feel... well, makes me feel absolved from responsibility.

403
00:30:46,511 --> 00:30:50,112
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's incredibly dangerous because of that, because of that sort of intoxication.

404
00:30:50,172 --> 00:30:52,732
[SPEAKER_03]: You willingly forsake your reason and discern it.

405
00:30:52,792 --> 00:30:55,293
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't realize, okay, it's because it's pleasurable.

406
00:30:55,413 --> 00:31:00,663
[SPEAKER_03]: And that happens to people, especially, like, when it comes to, you know, following the instructions of others in the religion.

407
00:31:01,612 --> 00:31:11,437
[SPEAKER_03]: On account of being intox... self-intoxicated by your own faith and avoiding responsibility, you neglected the obvious things that are wrong right in front of you.

408
00:31:11,457 --> 00:31:19,140
[SPEAKER_03]: You ignored, you turned a blind eye, and then later on, sure, when things went too far, then it's like, oh, wow, yeah, I don't know how that happened.

409
00:31:19,641 --> 00:31:26,664
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like this charismatic leader, he looks like he's... or she looks like they... they got it sorted, so I'll just, you know...

410
00:31:27,284 --> 00:31:29,426
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I'll just submit to nothing for myself.

411
00:31:31,628 --> 00:31:37,433
[SPEAKER_03]: And even if that charismatic leader is the Buddha himself, he won't get you anywhere.

412
00:31:38,554 --> 00:31:43,858
[SPEAKER_03]: And when people did just follow him because he was so charismatic and inspiring, he dismissed them, he told them off.

413
00:31:44,519 --> 00:31:48,261
[SPEAKER_03]: Told him to get lost, to stop following him, because they're not practicing what he said.

414
00:31:48,901 --> 00:31:52,483
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, those people, they didn't follow him, but actually fulfilled the teaching.

415
00:31:53,104 --> 00:32:03,509
[SPEAKER_03]: Like in the suttas, when an arahant, when a person becomes an arahant, they free their mind, it's always sort of portrayed with a description of, ah, the teacher has been worshipped.

416
00:32:04,109 --> 00:32:08,812
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they truly... That's the only way to pay respect to the Buddha, is to fulfill his teachings.

417
00:32:09,828 --> 00:32:11,429
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I paid my respects.

418
00:32:11,649 --> 00:32:14,691
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, I don't owe anything to anyone, including the Buddha himself.

419
00:32:16,933 --> 00:32:29,501
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, that's much, you know, more insecure, more full of doubt, requiring more of diligence, discomfort, endurance, than just, no, no, no, this is the greatest teaching, I'm a believer, I'm saved.

420
00:32:30,362 --> 00:32:34,825
[SPEAKER_03]: Say, what you mentioned now, you have doubt coming up, and you have these views coming up.

421
00:32:35,445 --> 00:32:37,987
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in light of

422
00:32:39,295 --> 00:32:43,178
[SPEAKER_03]: This discussion and this sutta, how do you deal with that?

423
00:32:43,879 --> 00:32:49,043
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you deal with the doubt and the mind and the insecurity and the anxiety and everything else?

424
00:32:49,824 --> 00:32:52,827
[SPEAKER_03]: Views regarding around that and lack of answers.

425
00:32:52,907 --> 00:32:56,330
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you do in terms of what we just described?

426
00:32:57,571 --> 00:33:02,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, like in terms of what you've just described, if you have been taking on the

427
00:33:04,057 --> 00:33:05,337
[SPEAKER_04]: See, the doubt as doubt.

428
00:33:05,397 --> 00:33:06,057
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you do that?

429
00:33:06,077 --> 00:33:06,617
[SPEAKER_03]: So, doubt will be

430
00:33:32,648 --> 00:33:37,231
[SPEAKER_01]: But wouldn't there be just another view that I have to do on account of the view?

431
00:33:37,512 --> 00:33:40,874
[SPEAKER_05]: Potentially, but if you're enjoying it on the right level... Aha!

432
00:33:41,194 --> 00:33:43,396
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's... that's what you do first.

433
00:33:43,596 --> 00:33:44,877
[SPEAKER_03]: You endure it on the right level.

434
00:33:46,038 --> 00:33:47,119
[SPEAKER_03]: What does that mean?

435
00:33:47,139 --> 00:33:51,322
[SPEAKER_03]: It means not turning... We're back onto the second chariot.

436
00:33:51,382 --> 00:33:53,183
[SPEAKER_03]: What does that mean in terms of the second chariot?

437
00:33:55,491 --> 00:33:59,053
[SPEAKER_03]: Enduring on the right level, what does that mean in terms of purification of the mind?

438
00:33:59,353 --> 00:34:02,674
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, citta, nimitta, yoniso, manasikāra... Purification of the mind.

439
00:34:04,014 --> 00:34:07,096
[SPEAKER_03]: So, greed, aversion, delusion, non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

440
00:34:07,856 --> 00:34:10,237
[SPEAKER_03]: What does that mean in those terms?

441
00:34:10,257 --> 00:34:11,197
[SPEAKER_03]: Enduring on the right level.

442
00:34:11,217 --> 00:34:12,718
[SPEAKER_05]: Not acting out of it.

443
00:34:12,778 --> 00:34:13,098
[SPEAKER_05]: Of what?

444
00:34:13,738 --> 00:34:17,740
[SPEAKER_05]: Of the doubt, of the unpleasant, the displeasure of the...

445
00:34:17,780 --> 00:34:23,402
[SPEAKER_03]: So, not acting out of greed or aversion or distraction in regard to discomfort of the doubt.

446
00:34:24,612 --> 00:34:30,319
[SPEAKER_03]: And you will be able to see that and not act out of it if you have been keeping the virtue and practicing sense of strength.

447
00:34:30,519 --> 00:34:31,099
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, otherwise...

448
00:34:31,240 --> 00:34:33,002
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's what you do, basically.

449
00:34:33,322 --> 00:34:40,851
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't engage with it on the level of the views, because, obviously, doubt will just feed more doubt if you act out of it.

450
00:34:41,291 --> 00:34:44,014
[SPEAKER_05]: Which is not sīla, which is not the first chariot.

451
00:34:44,074 --> 00:34:44,314
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

452
00:34:44,354 --> 00:34:45,716
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not along those principles.

453
00:34:45,796 --> 00:34:49,260
[SPEAKER_03]: So, of course, you might, you get, but then, wait, what do I do?

454
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:49,680
[SPEAKER_03]: What do I do?

455
00:34:49,700 --> 00:34:51,522
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I do what I have been doing.

456
00:34:52,183 --> 00:34:56,768
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is this intention rooted in, in this particular instance of this doubt or this anxiety?

457
00:34:56,828 --> 00:34:57,809
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I want to get rid of it.

458
00:34:58,693 --> 00:35:08,680
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why that other sutra says, you know, how does the monk free himself from hindrances, so from from mind kind of obsessed with sensuality, or your will, or doubt.

459
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:13,283
[SPEAKER_03]: Not by doing this or that, but by seeing through with yoniso manasikāra.

460
00:35:13,483 --> 00:35:20,588
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that which hinders you in terms of the sensual desire, you see through it and recognize that, oh, there's a beautiful object,

461
00:35:21,028 --> 00:35:23,810
[SPEAKER_03]: And pleasure in regard to it, that I'm acting out of.

462
00:35:23,990 --> 00:35:26,452
[SPEAKER_03]: Or in this case, oh, I must get rid of it.

463
00:35:26,652 --> 00:35:32,217
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, so there is this beautiful object that besets me, and aversion towards it, that I'm acting out of.

464
00:35:33,060 --> 00:35:38,262
[SPEAKER_03]: So, by seeing through with that proper attention, he re-establishes the context.

465
00:35:38,762 --> 00:35:40,443
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's what the Buddha was describing as well.

466
00:35:40,463 --> 00:35:48,665
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, in... we mentioned a number of times lately that the whole... the two types of thoughts that he decided to think wholesome, not unwholesome.

467
00:35:49,145 --> 00:35:53,987
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, when unwholesome comes up, so when hindrances come up, oh, I'll just...

468
00:35:54,047 --> 00:35:54,347
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll just...

469
00:35:54,387 --> 00:36:00,589
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll just focus on a part of my body until I forget about this.

470
00:36:00,609 --> 00:36:02,430
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you won't find that in the suttas for sure.

471
00:36:03,415 --> 00:36:24,251
[SPEAKER_03]: What he did say he was doing was... ah, so when there is the thought of sensuality, or cruelty, or real will, and you could even say doubt or anxiety, he... that was there, but he did not attend to it wrongly, at the face value, with the attitude, I must get rid of this, I must act out of this, aversion towards this.

472
00:36:24,732 --> 00:36:29,496
[SPEAKER_03]: Instead, no, I would just keep investing my effort on the level of the context.

473
00:36:29,616 --> 00:36:31,978
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this thought of sensuality that's here,

474
00:36:32,910 --> 00:36:37,373
[SPEAKER_03]: Has it been kind of leading to my harm or my benefit in the past?

475
00:36:37,654 --> 00:36:38,614
[SPEAKER_03]: How about in the present?

476
00:36:38,775 --> 00:36:40,536
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it for my good or not?

477
00:36:41,136 --> 00:36:42,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it for benefit of others?

478
00:36:42,818 --> 00:36:43,458
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it helpful?

479
00:36:43,838 --> 00:36:44,559
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it pressuring?

480
00:36:45,140 --> 00:36:46,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it increasing of the weakness or the strength?

481
00:36:47,121 --> 00:36:48,902
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it increasing of dispassion or passion?

482
00:36:49,803 --> 00:36:55,067
[SPEAKER_03]: Just by pondering on those things, in face of the sensual desire, sensual desire will subside.

483
00:36:55,367 --> 00:36:57,149
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that is really what it is.

484
00:36:57,869 --> 00:36:59,751
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the real context of the sensuality.

485
00:37:01,215 --> 00:37:03,356
[SPEAKER_03]: Ill-will, cruelty, doubt.

486
00:37:04,136 --> 00:37:17,020
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, sure, it would be important to know this, but I'm obviously now emotionally gripped by doubt and I want to get rid of the pain of it, which means any answers I'm seeking on account of doubt in mind will be secondary.

487
00:37:17,280 --> 00:37:22,022
[SPEAKER_03]: All I'm doing is fueling the aversion towards pain and that's a hindrance in itself.

488
00:37:23,082 --> 00:37:23,662
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what do I do?

489
00:37:23,722 --> 00:37:24,402
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I attend...

490
00:37:25,203 --> 00:37:28,483
[SPEAKER_03]: I attend to it rightly or I see through it, pretty much.

491
00:37:28,964 --> 00:37:32,244
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there is this doubt that at a face value is trying to make me act.

492
00:37:32,705 --> 00:37:35,105
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't act, I endure it and I see what's through it.

493
00:37:35,265 --> 00:37:42,047
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, aversion to pain is the goal of this seemingly seeking for an answer that my mind is trying to overwhelm me with.

494
00:37:42,107 --> 00:37:45,468
[SPEAKER_03]: Whenever I was averse and acting out of pain, how did that result?

495
00:37:46,212 --> 00:37:48,173
[SPEAKER_03]: Did that bring pain to increase or decrease?

496
00:37:48,333 --> 00:37:50,554
[SPEAKER_03]: Did that overcome doubt once and for all or not?

497
00:37:51,094 --> 00:37:52,855
[SPEAKER_03]: Did it lead to my harm, harm of others?

498
00:37:52,875 --> 00:37:54,695
[SPEAKER_03]: Did it lead to composure or distraction?

499
00:37:55,216 --> 00:37:57,797
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, look, doubt is gone, because I basically...

500
00:37:57,817 --> 00:38:04,760
[SPEAKER_03]: I undone the attitude that was underlying, hidden behind this doubt that I was trying to get rid of.

501
00:38:04,960 --> 00:38:10,542
[SPEAKER_05]: But asking those questions that you've asked would be impossible if you're acting out of it, if you're...

502
00:38:10,942 --> 00:38:12,583
[SPEAKER_05]: Being absorbed in the content.

503
00:38:12,603 --> 00:38:12,763
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

504
00:38:12,944 --> 00:38:19,048
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody, again, somebody might hear this now, and they have experience of doubt, and then they say, I must enact out of doubt.

505
00:38:19,408 --> 00:38:21,169
[SPEAKER_03]: I must do what this ajahn said.

506
00:38:21,269 --> 00:38:24,672
[SPEAKER_03]: And they start asking these questions, rooted in trying to get rid of doubt.

507
00:38:24,712 --> 00:38:24,892
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

508
00:38:25,392 --> 00:38:33,298
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, they will not be able to see through that and abandon that, unless they have already been purifying their mind on account of taking responsibility and purifying their virtue.

509
00:38:33,558 --> 00:38:34,479
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it doesn't work.

510
00:38:34,879 --> 00:38:38,782
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it stays on the level of abstraction and abuse, even if it isn't so.

511
00:38:40,485 --> 00:38:55,514
[SPEAKER_03]: But those people who have taken responsibility for the virtue, purified the virtue, accomplished themselves in it, purified the intentions behind their actions, purified their mind, developed samādhi on account of it, they will catch this pretty much soon, if not immediately.

512
00:38:56,354 --> 00:38:57,335
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, wait!

513
00:38:57,355 --> 00:39:02,438
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, yeah, I shouldn't be getting rid of the doubt, but now I'm telling myself that I shouldn't be getting rid of the doubt in order to get rid of the doubt.

514
00:39:03,018 --> 00:39:04,920
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, I will not act out of that intention.

515
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:08,862
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I'll let doubt be there and I'll keep seeing through it.

516
00:39:10,643 --> 00:39:14,865
[SPEAKER_03]: And by seeing through it, the mind becomes unhindered from that which hinders you.

517
00:39:16,786 --> 00:39:24,889
[SPEAKER_03]: But it hinders you not because of itself, in and of itself, it hinders you because you had no perspective in regard to it.

518
00:39:25,369 --> 00:39:29,671
[SPEAKER_00]: It relates very much to faith as well, the right type of faith.

519
00:39:30,051 --> 00:39:35,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, for example, when you have... when there is doubt,

520
00:39:37,228 --> 00:39:38,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even any of the hindrances, actually.

521
00:39:39,730 --> 00:39:56,960
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to not act out of that doubt, because, like, your whole mind and everything that you've been doing for years and years, and your only way of finding some solution to it, or finding, this will give me confidence, this will give me faith, this will give me more, you know, what I need in order to keep going.

522
00:39:58,321 --> 00:40:03,725
[SPEAKER_00]: That, you know, I need to find the answer to this, or I need to seek a reassurance, or something.

523
00:40:05,362 --> 00:40:08,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And you need to not do that.

524
00:40:08,343 --> 00:40:14,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you need to, at some point, not seek safety in the things that you've been used to seeking safety in.

525
00:40:14,606 --> 00:40:14,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

526
00:40:14,786 --> 00:40:16,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no... Why?

527
00:40:20,064 --> 00:40:22,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't lead to safety.

528
00:40:22,165 --> 00:40:22,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

529
00:40:22,505 --> 00:40:25,105
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's rooted in already being unsafe.

530
00:40:25,205 --> 00:40:35,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And it actually only... what you are... you know, the more you're asking for something to give you faith, the more you are actually diminishing your possibility of having any faith.

531
00:40:35,988 --> 00:40:38,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the point.

532
00:40:38,849 --> 00:40:43,370
[SPEAKER_03]: The more of like... the more in a direction of non-responsibility you're traveling.

533
00:40:43,550 --> 00:40:45,771
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you're avoiding it and avoiding it.

534
00:40:45,791 --> 00:40:48,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You're like cultivating doubt, not cultivating faith.

535
00:40:48,491 --> 00:40:48,771
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

536
00:40:49,251 --> 00:41:04,335
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's very... and that's why faith is absolutely necessary, because there is nothing else, no external, no, you know, nobody else or nothing else can, you know, do it for you.

537
00:41:04,595 --> 00:41:07,156
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no confirmation except your own.

538
00:41:07,776 --> 00:41:09,976
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I think it pretty much would come down to that, wouldn't it?

539
00:41:10,216 --> 00:41:12,877
[SPEAKER_03]: Even it's like, you know, like we talk about enduring things.

540
00:41:14,258 --> 00:41:19,683
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, people understand that, but a number of people would not even understand that.

541
00:41:20,924 --> 00:41:24,888
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you read the suttas and say, well, no, it's about dealing and doing stuff.

542
00:41:24,928 --> 00:41:28,532
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, yeah, but how you go about it matters.

543
00:41:29,032 --> 00:41:37,661
[SPEAKER_03]: When you say you need to endure things without acting out of it, basically, yeah, you need a degree of faith to not act out immediately.

544
00:41:38,887 --> 00:41:40,169
[SPEAKER_03]: Just, just to that extent.

545
00:41:40,589 --> 00:41:42,993
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you see, okay, this is what I do.

546
00:41:43,013 --> 00:41:46,938
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't replay with thoughts of ugly, and ugly, and ugly, and concentrated nostrils.

547
00:41:47,599 --> 00:41:47,759
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

548
00:41:48,280 --> 00:41:49,201
[SPEAKER_03]: I see through it.

549
00:41:50,122 --> 00:41:54,308
[SPEAKER_03]: I see that this is just a beautiful object, inducive of lust, for which I'm responsible.

550
00:41:55,009 --> 00:42:00,111
[SPEAKER_03]: When you re-establish the context, the beautiful object remains standing there in the world, as the Buddha said, but there is no...

551
00:42:00,211 --> 00:42:00,631
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

552
00:42:01,311 --> 00:42:10,175
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you haven't had that initial faith to endure and not act out of this duty, you know, this is what I must do and address, which is all rooted in aversion towards that which making you feel uncomfortable.

553
00:42:11,572 --> 00:42:14,134
[SPEAKER_03]: So, without that faith, you just won't be able to restrain.

554
00:42:14,394 --> 00:42:15,395
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't be able to not act.

555
00:42:15,495 --> 00:42:17,716
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, even if you're doing the enduring, you're doing it.

556
00:42:18,017 --> 00:42:30,765
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you have faith, though, and not act out, and stay in that seemingly overwhelming position of, but I'm not dealing with this, but it needs to be dealt with, I need to endure it, out of faith, well, then the path can become apparent.

557
00:42:31,065 --> 00:42:37,850
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it's said even in the suttas, I think so in the Gūtara, that like a noble disciple, so one with the right view, maybe even sakadāgām

558
00:42:38,570 --> 00:42:44,674
[SPEAKER_03]: He has not become an anāgāmī yet, because he lacks faith, to a degree.

559
00:42:45,334 --> 00:42:47,576
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, he doesn't lack faith in the Buddha and the teachings.

560
00:42:47,876 --> 00:42:56,642
[SPEAKER_03]: But where does he... Well, he lacks faith, just to that kind of initial degree, to not act out of sensual thoughts or thoughts of ill-will.

561
00:42:57,182 --> 00:43:01,865
[SPEAKER_03]: To that extent, he's not freeing himself from sensual thoughts and thoughts of ill-will, because he acts out of them.

562
00:43:02,863 --> 00:43:06,149
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if not necessarily acting towards sensuality, he acts out of it.

563
00:43:06,189 --> 00:43:13,922
[SPEAKER_03]: He distracts himself, he dismisses it, he contemplates a subha to get rid of the pain behind the pressure of sensuality, not to just see the nature of sensuality.

564
00:43:15,392 --> 00:43:24,017
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why I keep saying, like, it's actually better if you practice a subha or loving-kindness before your mind is gripped with lust or your will.

565
00:43:24,337 --> 00:43:25,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Not in response to it.

566
00:43:26,118 --> 00:43:30,460
[SPEAKER_03]: And when I say practice before, I don't mean as a mindless technique and motion you go through.

567
00:43:30,961 --> 00:43:31,521
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, no.

568
00:43:32,882 --> 00:43:38,765
[SPEAKER_03]: Invoke the image of beautiful that would otherwise induce lust and start seeing that within that.

569
00:43:38,805 --> 00:43:42,047
[SPEAKER_03]: Start seeing the ugly, the perilous, the dangerous, the hidden trap and the bait.

570
00:43:43,307 --> 00:43:48,810
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the context needs to be developed sufficiently enough so that it doesn't move when these thoughts are present.

571
00:43:49,611 --> 00:43:51,212
[SPEAKER_03]: And the context moves when you act out of it.

572
00:43:51,692 --> 00:43:56,895
[SPEAKER_03]: And you act out of it when you don't have enough faith to endure the thoughts that are present without acting out of it.

573
00:43:57,936 --> 00:44:04,860
[SPEAKER_03]: So, to that extent, a noble disciple, if he lacks the faith that would enable him to not immediately

574
00:44:05,968 --> 00:44:14,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Resolve the sensual desire, or get rid of the ill-willed thoughts, he won't be free from those things, because he's perpetuating them through his ignorant action.

575
00:44:16,378 --> 00:44:28,911
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it should be obvious to anybody listening to this or our talks, when we talk about endurance, it's not on the same level as bearing of the elements, you know, like the Yadu Suttas, they said, it's translated as endurance, but it's it's not the same word I'm talking about.

576
00:44:29,679 --> 00:44:33,802
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, also, endurance means I just bear things mindlessly like that sutta says.

577
00:44:34,102 --> 00:44:38,044
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you bear heat, cold, thirst, words of others mindlessly, sure.

578
00:44:38,524 --> 00:44:41,806
[SPEAKER_03]: When we talk about endurance, you realize, oh, it's a completely different level.

579
00:44:42,007 --> 00:44:45,769
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the endurance that pertains to abandoning all of the taints in that sutta.

580
00:44:46,269 --> 00:44:50,592
[SPEAKER_03]: First, not acting out, having faith, and the gradual training.

581
00:44:52,133 --> 00:44:54,514
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the whole point of that would then be like, okay,

582
00:44:56,240 --> 00:44:58,465
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is in me being affected.

583
00:44:59,126 --> 00:45:04,397
[SPEAKER_03]: But if I act out of directly towards these things that affect me, I'm perpetuating the fact that I'm being affected.

584
00:45:05,977 --> 00:45:12,720
[SPEAKER_03]: So, faith as a factor, then, it's not just existing in somebody before he becomes a sotāpanna and then he...

585
00:45:13,080 --> 00:45:31,328
[SPEAKER_03]: Faith is a spiritual power, as the Buddha called it, which means, later on, you use the same faith to not act out of things immediately, to not get rid of them immediately, so that you can endure them, because already now you have confidence that enduring them on this level will lead to seeing through them.

586
00:45:32,173 --> 00:45:37,304
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's when you cease to be hindered by any of the things that are otherwise hindrances.

587
00:45:38,678 --> 00:45:41,000
[SPEAKER_03]: That does not mean that those things cease to arise.

588
00:45:41,080 --> 00:45:44,863
[SPEAKER_03]: It just means you're incapable of not seeing through them when they arise.

589
00:45:45,243 --> 00:45:46,424
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing them as hindrances.

590
00:45:46,584 --> 00:45:48,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing... Well, no, they're not hindrances anymore.

591
00:45:48,585 --> 00:45:49,126
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the point.

592
00:45:49,886 --> 00:45:53,069
[SPEAKER_03]: Seeing them as what used to hinder you when you attended to it wrongly.

593
00:45:53,089 --> 00:45:59,473
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it says, if he does not attend rightly to the sign of the beautiful, sensual hindrance.

594
00:45:59,954 --> 00:46:03,556
[SPEAKER_03]: If he attends rightly to the sign of the beautiful, no sensuality hindrance.

595
00:46:04,197 --> 00:46:05,758
[SPEAKER_03]: And same goes for all the other hindrances.

596
00:46:10,302 --> 00:46:16,767
[SPEAKER_03]: So, enduring things rightly would then result in purification of the views that will make you go beyond doubt.

597
00:46:18,008 --> 00:46:31,299
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's done on account of the second character, as we said, not acting out of intentions rooted in aversion, sensuality, ill-will, greed, all sorts of kind of layers of the same three principles.

598
00:46:31,319 --> 00:46:32,240
[SPEAKER_03]: Distraction as well.

599
00:46:32,660 --> 00:46:37,965
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, distraction is also, like, quite often ends up being justified means of dealing with things.

600
00:46:39,097 --> 00:46:45,419
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll focus on sensations, so I'm distracted from the pressing image that bothers me and emotionally disturbs me.

601
00:46:46,980 --> 00:46:52,341
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll go in a state of, you know, neither here nor there, half asleep, because that's peaceful.

602
00:46:52,681 --> 00:46:55,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Not thinking about what I'm subjected to is peaceful.

603
00:46:56,262 --> 00:47:01,024
[SPEAKER_03]: And people then conflate the successful meditation practice with successful degree of ignoring the problem.

604
00:47:03,245 --> 00:47:07,206
[SPEAKER_05]: This endurance that we're talking about, I think, is also integral to

605
00:47:08,745 --> 00:47:34,372
[SPEAKER_05]: Mindfulness, in the sense that... like mindfulness of the body, mindfulness of feeling, they... these are discerned as a relationship to what is presently enduring, in terms of... if you... if you don't... if you think of mindfulness of the body in terms of turning that into an object, like my body is an object, that is not seeing the relationship between

606
00:47:35,372 --> 00:47:43,098
[SPEAKER_05]: Whatever is, you know, occupying... whatever is in front of me, being determined by my beating heart or... How do I feel?

607
00:47:46,347 --> 00:47:49,948
[SPEAKER_03]: While I'm seeing this, how do I feel while I'm hearing these words?

608
00:47:50,988 --> 00:47:53,489
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I feel while I'm bearing heat and cold?

609
00:47:54,149 --> 00:47:57,670
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I enduring the feeling on account of bearing the elements?

610
00:47:57,790 --> 00:48:00,831
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's the relationship between the feeling and the bearing.

611
00:48:00,851 --> 00:48:04,893
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't find the feeling in your senses where you're observing things.

612
00:48:05,293 --> 00:48:12,695
[SPEAKER_03]: So, unless you need to look at that background, peripheral, in regard to what's there in front of you, there is no mindfulness for you.

613
00:48:13,435 --> 00:48:26,365
[SPEAKER_02]: In regards to not being, when you said not being able to act in certain ways, does that mean basically understanding wholesome as wholesome, or harmful as harmful, and beneficial as beneficial?

614
00:48:26,865 --> 00:48:29,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Because why would one be unable to act?

615
00:48:30,168 --> 00:48:34,591
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you haven't been acting long enough, and you took responsibility for avoiding that act.

616
00:48:35,612 --> 00:48:36,153
[SPEAKER_03]: Out of faith,

617
00:48:37,481 --> 00:48:39,502
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, out of seeing, you benefit directly for yourself.

618
00:48:39,542 --> 00:48:39,742
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

619
00:48:40,182 --> 00:48:42,742
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, you're stopping, basically, to see... Well, yeah, of course.

620
00:48:42,802 --> 00:48:47,803
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, the other six chariots don't come after the first one.

621
00:48:47,823 --> 00:48:49,304
[SPEAKER_03]: They're already waiting there.

622
00:48:49,724 --> 00:48:52,345
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you see what's in front of you, if you travel through this.

623
00:48:52,965 --> 00:48:57,106
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you are already seeing the purification of your intentions on account of purifying your virtue.

624
00:48:57,826 --> 00:49:01,687
[SPEAKER_03]: You are seeing the purification of views later on, if you keep purifying your virtue.

625
00:49:03,843 --> 00:49:07,485
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's a... it's a good... it's... again, it's a good criteria to go by.

626
00:49:07,705 --> 00:49:25,919
[SPEAKER_03]: Ask yourself, if, say, there is no authority, there is no immediate, like, tradition, family, even the law, would I still uphold the value

627
00:49:27,428 --> 00:49:32,573
[SPEAKER_03]: Of sense-restraint, guarding of the sense-doors, not acting out and not breaking the precepts.

628
00:49:33,154 --> 00:49:36,617
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I choose to do it and I've been choosing to do it for so long.

629
00:49:37,698 --> 00:49:42,703
[SPEAKER_03]: But there is no other thing to prevent you from doing it?

630
00:49:42,723 --> 00:49:43,484
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, there is.

631
00:49:43,524 --> 00:49:44,185
[SPEAKER_03]: Fear of jail.

632
00:49:45,357 --> 00:49:52,880
[SPEAKER_03]: Being beaten up, retribution, being kicked out of what you deem to be yours, which you take part, all these things prevent you from doing it.

633
00:49:53,380 --> 00:49:54,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Losing your life, too, doesn't count?

634
00:49:55,081 --> 00:49:56,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

635
00:49:56,121 --> 00:50:01,023
[SPEAKER_03]: They're all factual reasons, but none of those... all of those reasons, at the same time, yeah, it's good.

636
00:50:01,203 --> 00:50:06,705
[SPEAKER_03]: They didn't force you to be virtuous, but at the same time, they also take away responsibility for that virtue.

637
00:50:06,745 --> 00:50:11,127
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why virtue and duty, sīlabhāta-paramāsa, is a factual factor.

638
00:50:12,444 --> 00:50:14,746
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you need it, but you don't need it as a fetter.

639
00:50:15,126 --> 00:50:18,248
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the difference I'm describing.

640
00:50:18,308 --> 00:50:20,529
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't take responsibility, it will be a fetter.

641
00:50:20,649 --> 00:50:30,136
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you feel fully justified, because virtue is always good, well, you feel fully justified in just worshipping the first chariot and not even sitting in it, let alone reaching the second one.

642
00:50:31,016 --> 00:50:39,342
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is exactly why people who are not free from Sīla Bhāgavatam would not see the purification of the mind for what it is.

643
00:50:41,466 --> 00:50:51,987
[SPEAKER_03]: which is purification of intentions arisen at the time, not a blanket technique, concentration or approach that you can just have regardless of what state the mind is in.

644
00:50:54,834 --> 00:50:59,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, so are you independent of the concrete circumstances you find yourself in?

645
00:50:59,958 --> 00:51:08,764
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you can say yes to that, the more you are moving towards the noble ones, who are actually, factually independent, not only of circumstances, but even of the teachings.

646
00:51:08,804 --> 00:51:09,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly, exactly.

647
00:51:09,884 --> 00:51:16,209
[SPEAKER_03]: And the more your mind will be composed and gladdened and joyful on account of the unbroken virtue that I'm accomplished in.

648
00:51:16,829 --> 00:51:21,793
[SPEAKER_03]: So, whether I'm in the forest or in the city, things that I will not do,

649
00:51:22,920 --> 00:51:25,583
[SPEAKER_03]: Remain clear and kind of immovable.

650
00:51:25,983 --> 00:51:27,865
[SPEAKER_03]: The context of that remains immovable.

651
00:51:29,247 --> 00:51:30,708
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is something you train for.

652
00:51:30,969 --> 00:51:35,213
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not something that is, as again, people would kind of naively hope.

653
00:51:35,814 --> 00:51:39,137
[SPEAKER_03]: You take on, through a tradition, through an authority,

654
00:51:40,174 --> 00:51:46,683
[SPEAKER_03]: Do some sort of technique or practice or whatever, have a special experience and now you become unable to break your virtue.

655
00:51:46,883 --> 00:51:47,043
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

656
00:51:47,083 --> 00:51:53,953
[SPEAKER_03]: You become unable to break your virtue if you have cultivate non-breaking of it responsibly for sufficient period of time.

657
00:51:56,181 --> 00:52:02,427
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you start recognizing the factual samādhi that comes, the factual safety that comes as a result of it.

658
00:52:02,527 --> 00:52:05,750
[SPEAKER_03]: Not, oh, I was virtuous, so devas will protect me.

659
00:52:05,890 --> 00:52:06,590
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, they might.

660
00:52:07,531 --> 00:52:10,414
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's not something you see for yourself, nor can you see for yourself.

661
00:52:10,834 --> 00:52:15,278
[SPEAKER_03]: What you can see for yourself as a factual protection is mind unmoved.

662
00:52:16,968 --> 00:52:22,433
[SPEAKER_03]: Mind cannot act and resist out of aversion as much as it be.

663
00:52:22,473 --> 00:52:24,755
[SPEAKER_03]: Cannot turn against itself, as I was saying before.

664
00:52:25,335 --> 00:52:28,418
[SPEAKER_03]: Cannot turn around and bite me, if it's upset with the world.

665
00:52:29,058 --> 00:52:30,599
[SPEAKER_03]: Cannot give me sleepless nights.

666
00:52:31,320 --> 00:52:33,482
[SPEAKER_03]: Cannot give me suicidal depressive thoughts.

667
00:52:34,880 --> 00:52:47,528
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's been tamed, and it's been tamed by me, who's been avoiding acting in certain directions, and then guarding of the sense doors, and sustaining of the right context in regard to it.

668
00:52:47,628 --> 00:52:51,471
[SPEAKER_03]: I have been making the work, putting the work in there diligently.

669
00:52:52,111 --> 00:52:59,216
[SPEAKER_03]: As a result of it, the mind is calm, and you see it, and you have a direct verification of why this is worth doing.

670
00:53:06,810 --> 00:53:08,211
[SPEAKER_03]: So, not acting out, yeah?

671
00:53:08,751 --> 00:53:11,333
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's always the... comes down to that, the problem of it.

672
00:53:11,733 --> 00:53:13,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Pressure to act out, and then acting out.

673
00:53:13,974 --> 00:53:20,338
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, no, what I meant is that, like, you can't escape responsibility, basically.

674
00:53:20,358 --> 00:53:28,723
[SPEAKER_02]: You can not act in certain ways, because you don't want to go to jail, or don't want to be kicked out, or anything, but... You don't see anything more immediate.

675
00:53:30,331 --> 00:53:31,732
[SPEAKER_02]: It can make any difference.

676
00:53:31,872 --> 00:53:34,774
[SPEAKER_02]: You can always choose.

677
00:53:36,356 --> 00:53:39,638
[SPEAKER_02]: In regard to whatever you choose, it's you who chooses.

678
00:53:40,159 --> 00:53:40,839
[SPEAKER_03]: Theoretically, it's...

679
00:53:41,460 --> 00:53:41,620
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

680
00:53:42,139 --> 00:53:46,381
[SPEAKER_03]: If you speak about theory from the point of view of the third chariot, yes, but that's not seen.

681
00:53:47,181 --> 00:53:48,142
[SPEAKER_03]: One thing is to say that.

682
00:53:48,702 --> 00:53:53,904
[SPEAKER_03]: Another thing is, are you saying that on account of being already accomplished in virtue, when it will be factually true?

683
00:53:54,705 --> 00:53:55,805
[SPEAKER_03]: Now it's not factually true.

684
00:53:56,125 --> 00:54:01,668
[SPEAKER_03]: It's theoretically true, and it's good to strive for that, but it's not an answer, and it's not an explanation.

685
00:54:01,828 --> 00:54:03,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it is an explanation, but it's not an answer.

686
00:54:04,830 --> 00:54:05,390
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes.

687
00:54:06,635 --> 00:54:09,056
[SPEAKER_03]: You will be in the third chariot if you travel with the first two.

688
00:54:09,636 --> 00:54:11,457
[SPEAKER_03]: And you cannot not be in the third chariot.

689
00:54:11,597 --> 00:54:12,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, but are you?

690
00:54:13,638 --> 00:54:15,659
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's my point.

691
00:54:15,899 --> 00:54:20,901
[SPEAKER_03]: You are always doing it, it's always on you, but that's not seen and that's not how it's practically lived.

692
00:54:22,102 --> 00:54:29,565
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, now, I gotta do this, regardless of the environment, and the tradition, and the requirements, and the expectations.

693
00:54:31,377 --> 00:54:41,342
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, if you do it like that, then you will then see the benefit for yourself there and then, wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome is unwholesome, regardless of the environment, regardless of the tradition.

694
00:54:42,662 --> 00:54:55,068
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why it's said, the one, the Sotāpanna, who has freed himself from Sīla Bhakti Paramahāsa, gone beyond doubt, he, like, even if the whole world says, oh, the Buddha's instruction was wrong, he'll know that the world is wrong.

695
00:54:55,888 --> 00:54:59,670
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because he's so blinded by the faith in the Buddha, but his direct verification.

696
00:55:00,993 --> 00:55:13,676
[SPEAKER_03]: Amid suffering, amidst brink, basically, of existence, depression, overwhelming pain, he cannot suffer because of the purification he developed and the way he sees things now.

697
00:55:14,176 --> 00:55:17,757
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because of he got rid of all of this and nothing ever comes to bother him.

698
00:55:18,657 --> 00:55:23,118
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the confidence cannot be shaken because, I mean, this is it, like...

699
00:55:23,690 --> 00:55:32,317
[SPEAKER_03]: When amidst of the life-threatening pain and fear and anxiety, the mind does not turn against you, or itself, you don't suffer.

700
00:55:33,458 --> 00:55:37,501
[SPEAKER_03]: There is nothing greater that can verify your success, in terms of practice.

701
00:55:37,541 --> 00:55:39,022
[SPEAKER_03]: So, suffering and freedom from it.

702
00:55:39,082 --> 00:55:42,645
[SPEAKER_03]: That's all the Buddha teaches, and that's all he needs to teach, and that's all you need to know, really.

703
00:55:44,046 --> 00:55:45,547
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's what it all boils down to.

